From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sat, 02 Apr 94 01:13:48 GMT
Subject: [#94-4-10] Simulation vs. Reality - part 3: benefits of sentient machines

[This follows on from part 2]

Returning to the issue of time and consciousness, some useful possibilities
are opened up by the fact that the subjective "present" is not an
infinitesimal point in time.  In *Consciousness Explained*, Dennett
discusses the phi experiments of Kolers and von Grunau, and the "Multiple
Drafts" model of consciousness.  The phi experiments involved flashing two
different colored small spots which could be separated by up to 4 degrees
of the subject's visual field.  Say a red spot on the left is flashed for 
150 mS, followed by a 50 mS interval; then a green spot on the right is
flashed for 150 mS, followed by a 50 mS interval.  The subjects report
that the red spot appears to start "moving" sideways, and suddenly appears
to turn green halfway before it "arrives" at the green spot's actual
position.  (And this wasn't due to subjects learning to expect a green
flash; the same effect was reported right from the start, and it remained
even if the second spot's color and position was altered at random.)

The two most obvious explanations, short of precognition, are the
Stalinesque fabricated "show trial" mechanism, and the Orwellian historical
archives revisionist alternative.  The Stalinesque method requires
consciousness to lag real time by a few hundred milliseconds.  Somewhere
between the sensory inputs and the part of the brain where consciousness
all "comes together", there is sufficient delay for the brain's editors to
stage-manage the show before projection to the audience, inserting "moving
spots" and adjusting color as it thinks appropriate, from the data it
already has.  Alternatively, the Orwellian revisionist method has
subjective time synchronized with objective time, and the gap between
flashes is actually experienced; within a few hundred milliseconds the
brain's "Ministry of Truth" decides that the spot should have been moving,
so it rewrites memory to reflect this.  In one version, consciousness is
tampered with and memory is accurate; in the other, consciousness is
accurate but memory is rewritten.

But once we start dealing with times in the hundreds of milliseconds range,
the Stalinesque / Orwellian distinction becomes a false dichotomy.  Tests
involving the subjects having to press a button in response to experiencing
the first spot might appear to rule out a => 200mS delay; however, the
alternative hypothesis would propose that unconscious awareness of the
first spot initiates the button press, before vision of this spot has been
projected onto consciousness.  Both versions can satisfactorily account
for the data from the viewpoint of the experimenters and the subject, and
the distinction is non-operational.  

It is accepted by practically everyone that many streams of brain activity
operate in parallel, subject to a continual editorial processing as
features are detected and discriminated.  The old model assumed the
existence of a "special centre" in the brain, a master discriminator where
a "finished product of consciousness comes together" at a specific time.
In the Multiple Drafts model of consciousness this idea is dispensed with.
There is no point in the brain or moment of processing which could be
considered as the "moment of consciousness"; rather, it's better to think
of all these multiple "drafts" of each element of the consciousness stream
under various stages of revision at each point in time.  And subjective
time (after much of the editing has occurred) does not remain in the same
order as objective time.  The order of phenomenal time is set by the
brain's timestamps rather than the order in which the brain events occur.
But there is clearly a "window" of some 200 - 400 milliseconds which might
be considered "the present".

Many modern physicists believe that the only scientific, materialistic
ontological model is that of multiple universes.  All single universe
models must resort to dualism and/or mysticism, and are non-relativistic
and anti-reductionist.  In short, their credibility rating is very low.

The problem with trying to assign an active role for consciousness has
been in explaining how the "mind" can act on matter in a top-down sense,
without postulating a "ghost".  It's much easier to dismiss the mind-body
problem as no problem, and claim that "will" is an illusion.  And we have
to explain what a mind is.  A mind is the combination of sentience and
intelligence which emerges in the volume of space occupied by a suitably
working brain, existing across all those parallel worlds where the physical
states of the brains are similar enough not to cause a differentiation of
the consciousness stream.  The conscious mind is the sentient part, the
landscape in electromagnetic space; the unconscious mind is the running of
the algorithm.

Let's say a human being chooses to raise her right arm.  We take a slice
of time a few hundred milliseconds in duration, ending roughly at the time
when the decision appears to have been taken.  Throughout this period, her
mind occupies a myriad of brains, many of which are duplicates, with many
others differing in various ways, these distinctions being of such nature
that they are as yet unconscious.  Now, the decision has been taken to
raise the arm, and the brain unconsciously monitors the relevant action
potentials in the motor cortex.  (Of course, a great deal of the time, the
conscious mind is not involved in control of bodily movements -- the
individual just runs on autopilot, with the brain's pre-von Neuman
computer handling everything.)  Most likely, the required action potential
does not exist.

But due to quantum randomness, worlds exist where the action potential has
started to build up.  At the time when these spikes are still of low to
moderate amplitude, the distinction is at an unconscious level -- being
insufficient to cause divergence at the level of the conscious mind,
whilst becoming known to the unconscious mind.  It's easy to picture an
animal travelling through 3D space, coming up to a fork, then taking a
decision as to which road to take.  If it wants to turn right, it does so.
Now, if we think of the multitude of parallel worlds spanned by our
human's conscious mind as a multiverse, a segment of the whole of "reality
space" (the omniverse), then conscious decisions are equivalent to motion
across "reality space".  After the action potentials have built up to
sufficient amplitude, divergence of consciousness occurs, and the former
single multiverse has split into two.  Those where the subject chose to
raise her right arm, and those where she didn't.  And unfortunately, being
able to select between those worlds where you move a particular arm or
write a given set of words rather than another doesn't provide you with
magickal abilities, e.g. selecting those worlds where you won the lottery!

Take Libet's experiments involving subjects making decisions on when to
flex a hand at the wrist, or Kornhuber's where the subjects had to decide
suddenly to flex an index finger.  (These are sometimes claimed to "prove"
that we're just powerless spectators, incapable of ever making any
conscious decisions, since the action potentials preceded the apparent
time of the "conscious decisions" by a few hundred milliseconds.)  One
possible explanation would be that of the Orwellian revision to memory.
The conscious intention existed at the moment it was taken, was then
temporarily erased from consciousness and memory, reappearing a little
later with the revised timestamp.  A better explanation is that it's
consistent with a theory of many minds.

>From the subjects' point of view, they successfully end up in the myriad
of worlds (multiverse) where they made the decisions they did, and the
rising action potentials correctly preceded each decision.  The
experimenters, on the other hand, end up in some particular multiverse 
where each subject happened to make a particular set of decisions, and
the measured rising voltages correctly preceded each decision.  Once they
had knowledge of the timing of the decisions and electrical signals, they
could not remain in worlds where the measurements were incompatible --
this would be like observation of a cat which was simultaneously dead and
alive!

If we develop this further towards notions such as minds oscillating in
time, it can start to become too dualist and raise questions like "what
does it mean anyway?"; if these can be resolved it could provide further
explanation of how conscious decisions are taken, and provide a mechanism
for solving NP-complete problems in polynomial time.

Back in 1876, Loschmidt and others advanced the idea of the
"irreversibility paradox".  Lord Kelvin pointed out that living creatures
would rise from the dead, eventually becoming unborn, if the motion of
every particle in the universe were precisely reversed at any instant.  At
the macrocosmic level time clearly appears irreversible; at the microcosmic
level there is no evidence of irreversibility.  This doesn't invalidate
reductionism; we cannot observe macrocosmic behaviour at the microcosmic
level.  But luckily, particles just happen to exhibit the correct behaviour
so that top-down and bottom-up explanations are compatible.  It's generally
regarded that time "flows" unidirectionally at the macrocosmic scale.  At
the level of living creatures, I suggest it is not quite so unidirectional,
particularly over very short periods of time.  More like this:

Consider a sandy beach, with the tide coming in.  The sea is making 
inexorable progress towards the land, but due to the waves its advance
is a hundred steps forwards, ninety-nine backwards.  Or take a rat
progressing along a maze.  Sometimes it makes a mistake and goes
backwards temporarily, but it does eventually get to the end.  Or look at
the behaviour of stock market indices...

If our view of time flowing linearly at the subjective level does not 
apply to the unconscious mind, there could be important benefits.  We know
that the ensemble of worlds encompassed within an individual's multiverse
can't include her antimatter twin, since the two would be annihilated.
But maybe these antimatter universes can be of use for computational
purposes.  The conscious mind cannot discriminate between infinitesimal
units of time; at each point in the individual's experience there is a
moment deemed to be the "present".  Over any period, time-synchronized
antimatter universes are ruled out, since the polarity of the conscious
mind's electromagnetic landscape is reversed, hence the minds are distinct.
The same goes for time-reversed matter universes.  And over a period of
several "presents", time-reversed antimatter universes are clearly distinct
due to time-reversal.  But if the conscious mind takes a particular period
in time as the "present", time-reversed antimatter worlds could temporarily
be included within her multiverse.
 
So during this moment considered to be the "present", the unconscious mind
carries out a computation in the forwards direction in one of the matter
worlds, then runs backwards in time in a reversed antimatter world, then
forwards in time in a matter world...  The result is a superposition of
logical states in the brain's neural network, with an answer given -- i.e.
firing of the particular neural structure (meme) representing the perceived
shape -- during the "present".  Animals whose brains evolved to learn how
to do this would certainly be fitter than those who didn't.  And for 
enabling free will, we'd have something like this:

start:;
if required action potential does not exist then goto start
end of loop:;
advance conscious mind to next "present" by changing amplitude of currently
    activated memes sufficiently to become known to conscious mind

A totally random behaviour which was solely driven by quantum randomness
in a bottom up approach would be useless, as everyone agrees.  If you
have behaviour which is almost totally algorithmic-driven coupled with a
very small random component in each world, this allows the default
behaviour to be algorithmic-driven purposeful action, and ensures
sufficient range of worlds within each individual's multiverse to cater
for the range of possible conscious decisions.

The single universe mechanistic theories of the mind rule out any active
role for consciousness (although they have it playing a spectator's role
in "collapsing wavefunctions"), whereas multiverse alternatives do not.
Of course, the main problem is working out the additional mechanisms.  In
the end, all sensible theories have to be mechanistic; it's just that some
theories have additional mechanisms at a deeper level. 

