>Subject: [#94-4-7] Trouble With Left Anarchists
>
>Tim Starr says of left anarchists...
>
>> 2) They blame the market for failures caused by the State.
>
>Since they think property is coercive, and it's protected by the State,
>then the market and the State are pretty much the same thing for them.

This is what I mean by their exploitation theory of property.  They assume
common ownership of land, thus individual acquisition of it is considered
theft and protection of it also coercive.  Further, all capital is merely
the fruit of this initial act of aggression.  What I can't figure out is
why they make the initial assumption of common ownership.  While I know that
Dan Davis defends it on efficiency grounds, I don't think that position is
tenable.

>There is a grain of truth here.  Many aspects of the market (including
>the existence of big corporations) are the result of State intervention.
>We should help leftists understand that just because we are for
>"capitalism" doesn't mean we want to prop up status-quo "capitalists."

There's two things involved here: libertarian class theory and the theory of
the firm.  Leftists, anarchists or not, tend to implicitly accept Marxist
class theory: capital v. labor.  Curiously, they omit the other two primary
factors of production: entrepreneurship and land.  Actually, it's arguable
that only these two are primary, while capital and labor are either derivative
or useless without these.  Nowadays the socialist left tends to pit land and
labor against capital and entrepreneurship, but the rationale for this is
unclear to me.

Anyways, Marxist class theory was doomed by its dependency upon the labor
theory of value which Bohm-Bawerk exploded a century ago.  It never really
recovered, even though many socialists tried to develop sociological theories
to take its place.  Nowadays we see a plethora of competing class theories:
race v. race, male v. female, homosexual v. heterosexual, etc.  Hence the
evil demon of contemporary leftism, the Euro-American heterosexual male
capitalist-entrepreneur - and also the divisiveness of the Left once its
defining enemy is out of the picture.  Then, all of a sudden, the various
factions comprising the coalition of the Left turn on each other, each
carrying out its own jihad against each other.

All this chaos presents wonderful opportunity to introduce libertarian class
theory, based not upon one's relationship to the means of production a la
Marx, but one's relationship to the means of coercion.  Those who make their
livings by coercive means are in the ruling class, the political class, and
those who make their living by production are in the ruled, or economic, class.

If you accept some vestige of Marxist class theory, as leftists tend to, then
"capitalist" means: "controller of capital" - not "producer," as it means to
those of us who accept the libertarian class theory.

The other thing is that while it's true that corporations are legal fictions
conferred by the State, we must be careful not to make too much of this.
Trusts are not creatures of the State, and are capable of taking the place
of corporations in some ways, such as accumulators of capital.  Also, joint-
stock companies are quite possible without any charter of limited liability
from the State.  When confronted with people concerned with any tendencies
towards size increases in firms, I think we're better off arguing that this
is due to transaction costs which the State tends to increase.  The two
things we can do to decrease transaction costs are to roll back the State
and to encourage entrepreneurs to exploit opportunities to profit from
innovations that bring them down.

